Kuresu the Samurai grew up in a tiny village and always wanted to be a 
bushi ever since he could wield a shinai and spar with his father. His 
youth was dedicated to improving his mind and body. On his thirteenth 
birthday he received a vision from Amaterasu Okami saying that one day he 
would perform a great deed in her name.

A young Lord Sato took notice of the bold warrior and accepted him as his 
personal servant. One day Kuresu was called into the lord's chambers and 
was told he was honored with a mission from the emperor and Amaterasu 
Okami!

Kuresu was promptly slain by a swarm of soldier ants. The cycle of rebirth 
was merciful to Kuresu-sama many times, more than six hundred according to 
the ancient scroll known as the logfile. Each time he started out on the 
quest for an increasingly older Lord Sato and eventually Lord Sato's 
descendants, he would suffer the ignominy of choking all over his food, 
seppuku out of frustration at the hands of a mind flayer, slain by falling 
rocks only moments after entering the dungeons, tumbling through a hole 
wielding a stone bird, and many others too embarassing to mention.

Not all of his attempts were futile. Many times he labored through the 
Dungeons of Doom, Medusa had always fallen quickly, the final reward of the 
castle being hard won after hundreds of battles with other honorable bushi 
of an unknown lord. The sights and smells of Gehennom were no mystery to 
him for he had visited those forsaken lands on many occasions and he 
remained grateful that Amaterasu Okami never saw fit to condemn him to its 
depths.

On October 11th, early in the evening, Kuresu struck out once again to 
retrieve the Amulet of Yendor. Already he had misgivings about the quest 
but his faith remained steadfast in his lady and his lord. His humble 
katana had tasted the blood of savage minor races, unclean and undead 
denizens of the shadowlands in addition to mighty beasts of natural and 
unnatural origin. He was surprised to discover a wand of wishing when he 
acquired the title of ninja. Quickly he was outfitted in shining armor made 
from the skin of a dragon, became blessed with increased speed and the 
strength of a typhoon from enchanted boots and yugake and finally protected 
himself with a mystical tattered cape that became the bane of every 
spellcaster who deigned to vent their wrath upon him.

A humble shopkeeper sold him a gaijin katana which became something far 
greater once it was washed in the waters of the dungeon's many springs. He 
fell into a steady rhythm of killing and eating that only bolstered his 
abilities, imbuing him with many intrinsics that are only attributed to 
legendary kami and heroes of the past. Soon he acquired a simple ring that 
made eating almost unnecessary for the rest of his journey.

The gorgon fell speedily upon seeing her reflection in his armor and much 
deeper he came upon the fabled castle where he slew every last man and 
creature only to find another wand of wishing. Several wishes later, he had 
wiped out all undead sorcerors, all the creatures which once lived in the 
damnable waters of these depths, bizarre creatures that ate metal and 
drained the mystical ability of his weapons. He had many encounters with 
the squid-men and despite their tastes he still remembered how dangerous 
they could be and that they too must follow the others to the grave 
wholesale lest he fail and forget what he was seeking in the first place.

Yet another castle awaited him deep in the walls of the dungeon, a 
foreigner with dark hair and eyes sat upon a heap of gold commanding 
abominations to slay the thief. Infuriated at the insult, Kuresu regained 
his honor, washing away the insult with the blood of Croesus. The gold was 
then donated to the priest of Raijin in the underground village of the tiny 
people with enormous noses. Kuresu was truly protected from anything that 
may dare assail him.

Now he turned his eyes homeward, remembering his promise to slay Lord Sato 
the LXXVIII's mortal enemy Ashikaga Takauji the LXXVII who had stole the 
Tsurugi of Muramasa many years ago and passed it along to his bastard kin. 
Now a Kokushu, he was sent on his way to the unknown lands that contained 
only wolves and invisible assassins along with the pitiful ronin who 
claimed Takauji as their lord.

The Tsurugi of Muramasa did not dull in the intervening decades and 
remained as sharp and deadly as its weilder's mind. Several close calls 
later, nightmares of Kuresu laying in two pieces flashing before his eyes, 
Ashikaga's head was thrown to the crows and his body to the waves.

Being a forward thinking samurai, he realized the potential for using the 
original mystical sword given unto him by the amphibious kami. Sure it was 
a gaijin's toy but a formidable toy, especially after imbuing it with 
mystical blue light. The tsurugi, along with Orcrist and Sting and a ninja-
to named Dragonbane and Snickersnee, was left atop the lone altar to 
Amaterasu Okami he discovered during his quest.

If only he could remember why the bell was so important.

Brilliant blue potions and the remains of souls increased his power until 
he became a shogun. Soon he cut a swath through the Valley of the Dead then 
entered Gehennom. That's where he discovered that he was just as flammable 
as he was when he was a humble ronin. After all those hours, after all 
those fire ants and red nagas he never acquired fire resistance. He wrested 
the final charge from his wand of wishing for a sapphire ring that 
protected him from the fires of Gehennom. A dark lord bore a golden device 
made to hold candles and granted him the very first amulet of life saving. 
Many oni fell, hundreds of bakemono and goblins pricked at him like swarms 
of mosquitos but he never succumbed or showed pain in their presence. He 
slew a red dragon and ate its heart then realized he would never need that 
sapphire ring again. Soon Kuresu found the very bottom of the festering pit 
of hell. An industrious soul, he dug out the entire level until the floor 
shook under his feet. After much consideration he sought out the Wizard of 
Yendor.

When he finally touched the ancient foreign tome borne with frightening 
runes and images, he realized he did not want this to be carried with his 
gear. It wouldn't fit into his magical bag so he decided to only pick up 
the book for brief periods then kick it towards the ladders and stairs.

Soon he made his way to the vibrating square, it was surrounded by gold 
pieces so he wouldn't have to search once again. It was very dark so he put 
some candles into the golden branch then he rang the bell since he figured 
it must do something followed by attempting to read the blasphemous text. A 
moat rose from the floor, fiery pits surrounded him ready to burst forth 
pillars of flame and stairs descended ever deeper into the bowels of the 
earth.

The Priest of Moloch died noisily and dishonorably, leaving the final prize 
for him.

What seemed like hours later, he finally reached his point of origin and 
remembered he owned two magic lanterns. Figuring one more wish wouldn't 
hurt, he rubbed the blessed one and was greeted by a vast saracen from the 
west who thundered "It's about time!"

Annoyed but far from dejected, he ascended to the elemental planes. Feeling 
cocky, he removed the amulet of life saving and wore the Amulet of Yendor. 
The planes were far from frightful despite the horror stories and visions 
of his past incarnations. Fortunately the amulet led him towards each 
portal. Earth had him crashing through dirt and stone until he stumbled 
upon the portal to the plane of air. Fearsome air elementals relentlessly 
battered him requiring him to use only one of his potions of full healing. 
Soon he was surrounded by fire in the plane of, well, fire. He blinded 
himself to prevent being blinded and distracted by the foibles of 
salamanders, fire giants and vortices. The plane of water was quiet and 
very simple as long as he remained patient and followed the bubble's 
seemingly aimless wanderings.

A flash of light and disorientation.

He fell hard onto the ground of the astral plane, an angel appeared beside 
him and granted him fealty as protector in this final endeavor. Angels of 
bastard gods, swarms of insects and ruthless gaijin who also sought the 
Amulet of Yendor set upon him. By pure chance he decided he would turn left 
and head in that direction.

Ron the Tourist fell quickly as did the Angels of Moloch and Susanowo. He 
spied the priestess and ran inside followed by a skeletal man bearing a 
scale in his hand. His valiant Angel of Amaterasu Okami, Corky, was 
surrounded by fiends. Honor required him to protect his newfound vassal but 
he quickly realized it would be far worse to die when so close to his goal. 
The angel looked at him and urged him onward.

He offered the Amulet of Yendor upon the altar of Amaterasu Okami and heard 
an invisible choir sing as he was bathed in radiance.

The voice of Amaterasu Okami rang out, "Congratulations, Mortal! In return 
for thy service, I grant thee the gift of Immortality!"

Kuresu ascended to demigodhood, escaping the cycle of rebirth forevermore.

